{"id": 33, "ts": 1781207012.26486, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "sarah_mitchell", "message": "I remain convinced that Candidate\u202fC is our best fit. As James and Emily have highlighted, C is 100\u202f% reliable, keeps calm in a crisis, makes correct decisions quickly, and creates a positive crew atmosphere\u2014exactly the mix of reliability, stress\u2011resilience, decision quality, and crew cooperation we need for long\u2011distance flights. The only remaining reservation is the limited evidence on C\u2019s attention\u2011to\u2011detail and adaptability, but those gaps are less critical than the clear lack of decision\u2011quality data for B and the crew\u2011cooperation risks we see with D.\nThat said, we still haven\u2019t examined Candidate\u202fA in depth. A can anticipate dangerous situations, sees complex connections, has excellent spatial vision, and shows very good leadership qualities\u2014attributes that could translate into strong technical competence and decision\u2011making. However, A is also noted as sometimes not good at taking criticism and can be unorganized, raising concerns about operational reliability and adaptability. Until we compare these points directly with C\u2019s profile, I recommend we keep C as the provisional lead while we bring A into the discussion to confirm that no hidden strength outweighs C\u2019s overall safety fit."}
